So pavement ring: inner radius just outside the blend (~2.3 normal, 2.9 crystal), outer ~4.6 normal / 5.6 crystal. Since I'll draw a full disc under everything at y≈0.015 with the brick shader, the shadow plane (y=0.02) will overlay inner part (dark AO) ✓ nice: bricks visible outside the AO. Order: add pavement BEFORE `_add_base_blend` or after? Both at nearly same y; the shadow plane is alpha-blended unshaded, so ordering by depth is coplanar-ish; set pavement y=0.012 and shadow y=0.02 → shadow drawn above ✓ (depth test). Since both are at slightly different heights, fine either way. To be safe, add pavement first (before `_add_base_blend(f)`) so draw order is pavement then shadow.

Full disc radius: normal 4.6, crystal 5.6 — but a *full* disc under the tower is hidden by the tower base; fine, only margin visible ✓ simpler than an annulus.

Now the brick shader — write `_BRICK_CODE`:

```glsl
shader_type spatial;
render_mode diffuse_burley, specular_schlick_ggx;

uniform vec4 brick_a : source_color = vec4(0.34, 0.43, 0.45, 1.0);   # 青砖
uniform vec4 brick_b : source_color = vec4(0.26, 0.34, 0.36, 1.0);
uniform vec4 mortar_col : source_color = vec4(0.16, 0.19, 0.20, 1.0);
uniform vec4 moss_col : source_color = vec4(0.22, 0.32, 0.20, 1.0);
uniform float brick_w = 0.86;
uniform float brick_h = 0.42;

varying vec3 wp;

void vertex() { wp = (MODEL_MATRIX * vec4(VERTEX, 1.0)).xyz; }

void fragment() {
	vec2 p = vec2(wp.x, wp.z) / vec2(brick_w, brick_h);
	float row = floor(p.y);
	p.x += mod(row, 2.0) * 0.5;          // 错缝
	vec2 f = fract(p);
	vec2 id = floor(p);
	float edge = min(min(f.x, 1.0 - f.x), min(f.y, 1.0 - f.y));
	float mortar = 1.0 - smoothstep(0.02, 0.075, edge);
	float h = hash13(vec3(id.x, row, 0.0));
	vec3 col = mix(brick_a.rgb, brick_b.rgb, h);
	// 砖面微起伏明暗
	col *= 0.9 + 0.2 * hash13(vec3(id.x * 3.1, row * 1.7, 5.0));
	col = mix(col, moss_col.rgb, step(0.86, h) * 0.55);      // 少量青苔砖
	col = mix(col, mortar_col.rgb, mortar);
	ALBEDO = col;
	ROUGHNESS = mix(0.55, 0.95, mortar);
	SPECULAR = 0.35;
}
```
Need `__NOISE__` and `hash13` from `_NOISE_GLSL` ✓ reuse.

Now flowers material: StandardMaterial3D with vertex_color_use_as_albedo; need emission? Set `emission_enabled = true; emission = Color(0.25,0.2,0.2); emission_energy_multiplier = 0.15`? Simpler no emission.

Flower mesh builder: for a given variant, place ~7 blooms at random positions within radius 0.55, y 0.55..1.15 (on bush surface), each bloom = 3 quads? To keep cheap: each bloom = 2 crossed quads (petals) — but from top-down, horizontal quads read best. Let me do each bloom: 1 horizontal quad (0.16 size) + 1 small vertical? Simplest: horizontal quad slightly tilted + small yellow center quad above. Vertex colors: petals pink/white/violet, center yellow.

Quad helper:
```gdscript
static func _add_quad(st: SurfaceTool, c: Vector3, size: float, rot_y: float, tilt: float, col: Color) -> void:
	var b := Basis(Vector3.UP, rot_y) * Basis(Vector3.RIGHT, tilt)
	var h := size * 0.5
	var v := [Vector3(-h,0,-h), Vector3(h,0,-h), Vector3(h,0,h), Vector3(-h,0,h)]
	var n := (b * Vector3.UP)
	var pts: Array = []
	for i in [0,1,2, 0,2,3]:
		pts.append(v[i])
	for q in pts:
		st.set_color(col)
		st.set_normal(n)
		st.set_uv(Vector2.ZERO)
		st.add_vertex(b * q + c)
```
Hmm: SurfaceTool requires consistent attribute usage; setting color/normal/uv for every vertex ✓.

Material with `cull_mode = disabled` (CULL_DISABLED) so quads visible from below too.

Wall decor: ivy mesh = few leaf blobs (use `_leaf_blob()`) appended with `leaf_material()` ✓. Plus optionally a lantern: small emissive box (StandardMaterial3D emission warm amber 1.5) — I'll include a lantern as part of a second node only for some.

